Denmark, one of the smallest countries in Europe, is recognised worldwide for
its overall performance on the Sustainable Development Goals ( [ SDGs
](https://dashboards.sdgindex.org/rankings) ), climate [ mitigation
](https://ccpi.org/) policies, and its offshore [ wind farms
](https://www.reuters.com/article/us-climate-change-denmark-windpower-
idUSKBN1Z10KE) which expand into the North Sea.  1,2,3  According to research
published by [ Our World in Data
](https://ourworldindata.org/co2/country/denmark?country=~DNK#citation) , the
annual CO2 **emissions of Denmark have fallen by 40%** (Fig. 1) within the
last three decades (1990-2019).  4  This information has been published by the
[ Danish Energy Agency
](https://ens.dk/sites/ens.dk/files/Statistik/energistatistik2019_dk-
webtilg.pdf) 5  and it is distributed throughout the public and the media.
However, this article examines the other side of the coin and shows that this
description of climate policies does not represent the real story - or at
least, the whole story.

